#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512996} :activity/id #uuid "e9da63ab-03d4-5b28-8d6e-c384781de626" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048169 :topic/id #uuid "8546fff0-2cdf-5662-94b8-db689cb12a4a" :topic/math-academy-id 2096} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512979 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512865 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21b9f9b0-0c28-514b-949a-8339e5b0f60e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512171} {:answer/id #uuid "338b9ad0-884f-5a4e-8674-e7a8e1019500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512211} {:answer/id #uuid "7c4a6c4d-c28d-5c73-8281-c978daf12c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512393} {:answer/id #uuid "c61412bc-118a-5e93-b6c0-91732aceaf1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512576} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b7320f-6d58-56c5-83c3-fa0d4c1b8eca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512601}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512211} :answer-field/id #uuid "a756d2bc-643b-5bbc-b0e1-1d9618cee002" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "04de5946-03c2-52be-a91c-cc43fa9abf34" :question/math-academy-id "q-76450" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,2\\pi ],$ the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2\\pi ].$ Find $⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)⟩$ if the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )).$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-4t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+4t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)}{6}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12\\pi )}{6}]-\\frac{1}{2}[-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{6}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3dbec995-d7b1-5d9d-9fb5-ba6abfa94b3e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512984 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134385 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44900815-93d0-5419-bda4-15f71f1c2ebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327533} {:answer/id #uuid "8e2a67c3-6516-5ef0-8910-205fb5e42518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{t}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328165} {:answer/id #uuid "919c9679-b1d6-53d8-ba04-8bb16361e1db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{t}-t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328202} {:answer/id #uuid "b8bf4da2-ee9f-5281-99ba-7b938e201031" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328542} {:answer/id #uuid "d0bd2329-945b-550c-9f1d-b21cc4aa33ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328767}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328767} :answer-field/id #uuid "914fc28e-fc9f-53b3-abf8-9222f8795d35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "71904bfc-c560-50f5-ac2b-41eb0a528292" :question/math-academy-id "q-42256" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,2],$ the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2].$ Find $⟨t,{e}^{t}⟩$ if the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨t,{e}^{t}⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{t} & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for the integration by parts, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{0}^{2}-{\\int }_{0}^{2}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t{e}^{t}){|}_{0}^{2}-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2{e}^{2}-0{e}^{0})-{e}^{t}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}-({e}^{2}-1) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "512ebfd7-20c8-529c-8047-68f33115cdf7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513004}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512993 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512895 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33d9ec8b-a60d-5b77-ac5a-1965ca2efb92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512212} {:answer/id #uuid "34ee9bea-a7ee-5f69-b60d-dab66dd19aa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512217} {:answer/id #uuid "adf80400-9bf0-5756-9a8b-0e7694b4521f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512508} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ec2195-abc8-59fa-8eec-d947e7a76b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512536} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe9ee0f-144d-585c-9fe5-c21ac8f7efcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512720}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512217} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ffd9e21-a21c-5ae7-a7c7-e2f36c42374e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6a15f1db-7af4-5426-9f16-816905676768" :question/math-academy-id "q-76442" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2$, equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(0)y(0)+x(1)y(1)+x(2)y(2).\n$$\n\n Find $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$, if\n\n$$\nx(t)=t-1,\\,y(t)=t+2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Computing the inner product according to the definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =x(0)y(0)+x(1)y(1)+x(2)y(2) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-2+0+4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "63e80d05-6040-5f25-99b4-9ebfe7b7e44a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512979}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512996 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329639 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20ba600e-0358-513c-9008-d4164bd322af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327215} {:answer/id #uuid "7d5d4c0c-e9e9-5fde-8a0d-8854cd85cbf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328015} {:answer/id #uuid "8e92535a-d563-54ca-91ef-e0c126aef3fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328174} {:answer/id #uuid "ac342d67-73ca-57ea-b00b-d2af7ff07ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328429} {:answer/id #uuid "f88f22bf-caeb-53aa-96f2-904a0b368b2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329113}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328429} :answer-field/id #uuid "76480884-9973-5d4d-b09e-9119b17f3377" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "75517719-307f-569b-90da-09de54805f02" :question/math-academy-id "q-42255" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0],$ the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0].$ Find $⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,{cos}^{2}⁡t⟩$ if the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,{cos}^{2}⁡t⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{(1)^{3}}{3}]-[-\\frac{(0)^{3}}{3}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6ecbe002-c0f8-5d49-b600-f72c319b64d5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512984}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513004 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22514fb0-f6fc-5f81-86f8-4d2e1207589f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512173} {:answer/id #uuid "36b65fb5-0fee-5676-afc4-645c1b803bc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512220} {:answer/id #uuid "5261906e-cddb-5dc3-a97d-890d155fa5c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512281} {:answer/id #uuid "db85a92e-4361-5ea7-b58b-801f6714295d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512624} {:answer/id #uuid "e66a7f06-33be-5e84-baf2-c460003b0fda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512651}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512281} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ca6ff97-40bd-5746-ace1-2856a95eb89e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "890c5dfd-af10-56f3-b11c-f667a9950308" :question/math-academy-id "q-43813" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2$, equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(0)y(0)+x(-1)y(-1)+x(2)y(2).\n$$\n\nFind $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$, if\n\n$$\nx(t)=3+t-2{t}^{2},\\,y(t)=t-3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Computing the inner product according to the definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =x(0)y(0)+x(-1)y(-1)+x(2)y(2) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-3)+0\\cdot (-4)+(-3)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-9+0+3 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "93e68841-8807-59e4-aeea-be3ba4a3970a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512993}}] :activity/title "Inner Product Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512093}]}
